      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3653",
      ":question/problem": "Find $f''(x)$ for $f(x) = 2x^3 + \\dfrac 1 x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =2(3){x}^{2}-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2})^{′} \\\\  & =6(2)x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =12x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
