      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233123,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
        },
        ":question/id": "de3aadfd-ded1-53bb-8c7a-ca7c60560e8d",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-10907",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{''}(-1)$ if $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(4{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4})}^{'} &  \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4{x}^{4}-4\\cdot 3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(20{x}^{4}-12{x}^{3})}^{'} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 20{x}^{3}-3\\cdot 12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =80{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=-1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(-1) & =80(-1)^{3}-36(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-80-36 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233601,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
        },
        ":question/id": "1655c1ae-8739-50f2-81ee-c83924d8db9a",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-1852",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{''}(x)$ for $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={(12{x}^{3}-5\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{'} &  \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12{x}^{2}-5(\\operatorname{cos}x)+4(-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & ={(36{x}^{2}-5\\operatorname{cos}x-4\\operatorname{sin}x)}^{'} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 36x-5(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-4(\\operatorname{cos}x) \\\\  & =72x+5\\operatorname{sin}x-4\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
