        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-10892",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{″}(-2)$ if $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-7.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-7)}^{′} &  \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x-0 \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+8x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & ={(6{x}^{2}+8x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6x+8 \\\\  & =12x+8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=-2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+8 \\\\  & =-24+8 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
