        ":question/problem": "Find ${g}^{″}(1)$ if $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+4x\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${g}^{″}(1)=$ \n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(-{x}^{3}+4x\\sqrt{x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{3/2})}^{′} \\\\  & =3(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}(4{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}+6{x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2}+6{x}^{1/2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2(-3x)+\\frac{1}{2}(6{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-6x+3{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-6x+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & =-6(1)+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-6+3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
